<p><img decoding="async" src="https://www.futilitycloset.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/06/2026-06-24-around-the-block.png" alt="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cube_divided_into_eight_sub-cubes.jpg" width="500" height="467" class="aligncenter size-full wp-image-73579" srcset="https://www.futilitycloset.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/06/2026-06-24-around-the-block.png 500w, https://www.futilitycloset.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/06/2026-06-24-around-the-block-300x280.png 300w" sizes="(max-width: 500px) 100vw, 500px" /></p>
<p>From Arthur Engel&#8217;s excellent <em>Problem-Solving Strategies</em> (1998):</p>
<p>On a cube, mark all the vertices and all the midpoints of the faces. Can an enterprising ant visit all these points by walking only along face diagonals and never retracing its steps?</p>

